- A link's seal is the round's LAST seal, the base the next open reads. The loop seals many times per round (`append`'s cadence, `makeRoom`'s `sealNow`) and reopens only at `.fuel`. A mid-round seal is the END of a shorter life over the same open (folds concatenate), so every base the writer writes ends some `WriterLife` from its start and `writer_life_trusted` covers each; no single life lists every seal. The alternative shape (a link = open then a non-empty list of (fold, seal)) was considered and refused: its inner seals' premises would be carried and never consumed, a vacuity smell that buys no consumer (`life_opened_size_le` is about reopens, which read a round's last base; `life_refutes_potential` is about any reached base, a prefix life).
